From 04bd17c41660b9e943a655a109160aa5f2980c2f Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lccc-v15 <v15@localhost>
Date: Fri, 14 Aug 2026 17:32:34 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 4/6] =?UTF-8?q?PGO:=20stop=20regressing=20gzip=20=E2=80=94?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20neutral=20RA=20weighting,=2016-byte=20loop=20align,=20bisect?=
 =?UTF-8?q?=20gates?=
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Three measured regressions in the PGO use-mode pipeline, each fixed:

1. PGO-weighted register allocation (pgo_point_weights) inflated hot-block
   use counts by up to 4x, making hot-loop values near-unevictable. On gzip
   compress this produced +16 stack-slot accesses (~20%) inside longest_match
   and a +4.7% runtime regression vs plain -O2. The weighting also
   double-counts loop depth (priority already carries 10^loop_depth), so the
   default factor is now 1 (neutral); CCC_PGO_WEIGHT_MAX remains for
   experiments that show a real win.

2. The 32-byte alignment tier for very-hot loop headers bloated hot functions
   with multi-byte NOP padding (longest_match: 7 padding sites) and measured
   slower than 16-byte alignment. Only 16-byte alignment is now emitted,
   matching GCC's -falign-loops and LLVM's hot-loop default.

3. Add LCCC_PGO_NO_INLINE / LCCC_PGO_NO_UNROLL / LCCC_PGO_NO_PROMOTE
   diagnostic switches so each PGO consumer can be bisected in isolation.
